|mention4 =



|title5   = Her Words
|text5    = Sigrika stood at the front door of her childhood home, her hand resting on the handle. She didn't open it for a long time.  Ah... It was still hard. Hard to say everything out loud, to admit how tired she was, how unhappy. Even facing her own family, she found it difficult.  Or maybe... it was because they were her family that made it so hard.  After all, she didn't want them to worry.  "It's okay," she comforted herself. "If I can't even face my own family, how can I ever face myself?"  At last, she made up her mind and reached to turn the handle—but the door swung open on its own.  Her mother stood there, looking at her with helpless fondness. "Why didn't you come in?"  "Mother..."  "What's wrong?" her mother asked gently. "You've been out there for so long. Did something happen that you're afraid to tell me?" She smiled softly. "It's alright, we can take our time."  Sigrika stepped forward and wrapped her arms around her.  Something inside her crumbled all at once. Maybe it was the wall she had built, brick by brick, stone by stone, between herself and her family. Built from her pride. Her exhaustion. Her love. She had wanted so badly to be someone they could be proud of, so she never spoke of how tired she was. Never let it show. But now… she felt that she finally could.  "I've met some friends," she began. "Really good friends."  And there was so much more she wanted to tell them.
